5:## Using normative modeling to assess pharmacological treatment effect on brain state in patients with schizophrenia
23:Quantifying individual differences in neuroimaging metrics is attracting interest in clinical studies with mental disorders. Schizophrenia is diagnosed exclusively based on symptoms, and the biological heterogeneity makes it difficult to accurately assess pharmacological treatment effects on the brain state. Using the Cambridge Centre for Ageing and Neuroscience data set, we built normative models of brain states and mapped the deviations of the brain characteristics of each patient, to test whether deviations were related to symptoms, and further investigated the pharmacological treatment effect on deviation distributions. Specifically, we found that the patients can be divided into 2 groups: the normalized group had a normalization trend and milder symptoms at baseline, and the other group showed a more severe deviation trend. The baseline severity of the depression as well as the overall symptoms could predict the deviation of the static characteristics for the dorsal and ventral attention networks after treatment. In contrast, the positive symptoms could predict the deviations of the dynamic fluctuations for the default mode and dorsal attention networks after treatment. This work evaluates the effect of pharmacological treatment on static and dynamic brain states using an individualized approach, which may assist in understanding the heterogeneity of the illness pathology as well as the treatment response.
25:Key words : normative model; schizophrenia; pharmacological treatment; brain network; dynamic functional connectivity.
29:Schizophrenia is one of the most complex and heterogeneous mental disorders. The antipsychotic treatment is the first-line treatment for patients with schizophrenia. Many studies have suggested that the treatment significantly affects brain structure and function, yet individual differences in treatment responses are ubiquitous and prominent (Lin et al. 2021; Mehta et al. 2021; Matrone et al. 2022). Up to 30% of schizophrenia patients do not respond or respond poorly to standard treatment with antipsychotics. However, existing techniques usually operate at the group level to get an average profile of those who respond or do not respond to medical treatment, and provide no path to individual-level inference. To move away from group average statistics, neuroscientists and psychiatrists have adopted the normal growth charts approach, trying to quantify individual differences in brain and symptoms in patients suffering from mental disorders. As a promising approach, normative modeling posits that psychopathology characteristics of patients with the same diagnosis are not homogeneous, it highlights the value of understanding individual variation relative to group means (Marquand et al. 2016; Rutherford et al. 2022a, 2022b). Normative modeling has been applied in computational psychiatry, detecting individuals who deviate from the norm, including autism spectrum disorder (Bethlehem et al. 2020; Shan et al. 2022), major depressive disorders (Lin et al. 2023), Alzheimer's disease (Pinaya et al. 2021; Verdi et al. 2023), ADHD (Wolfers et al. 2020), and schizophrenia (Wolfers et al. 2018; Lv et al. 2020; Chien et al. 2022). Based on the normative model, this work aimed to examine how the deviations of the dynamic and static brain states are modulated by antipsychotic treatment, and whether the baseline severity of symptoms could predict the longitudinal changes.
31:Like the normal grow chart method, we must build a lifespan reference model to quantify individual variation against centiles of variation in a reference population to provide good lifespan coverage (Gur et al. 2014; Wolfers et al. 2020). Placing individuals into this coordinate system creates the opportunity to characterize their profiles of deviation. Recently, several studies tried establishing normative models charting brain structure lifespan trajectories and functional connectivity (FC; Rutherford et al. 2022a). Using 3 large data sets, Andre Marquand et al. (2016, 2019) established the normative model through different techniques and found that normative modeling features have the strongest statistically significant results when conducting group
37:difference statistics and classification tasks. Inspired by recent developments in mapping human brain charts, a recent study proposed that divergence between intra- and inter-person trajectory estimates varies with age and specific brain measures. They found that age-related brain changes inferred from crosssectionally mapped brain charts can substantially underestimate actual changes measured longitudinally (Di Biase et al. 2023). Motivated by this framework, psychiatrists have adopted the normative model in computational psychiatry (Oliveira-Saraiva and Ferreira 2023). In our previous study, deviations are quantified as having a z score with an absolute value larger than a threshold, and we found that compared with healthy controls (HCs), more patients with major depressive disorders showed deviation in the static and dynamic brain states (Lin et al. 2023), and we also identified a subtype of patients with larger extreme deviations from the normal range (Jing et al. 2023).
39:A previous study suggested that the normative model is suitable for longitudinal measurements in ascertaining brain development trajectories. Several studies have adopted a normative model to depict the distributions of patients with schizophrenia in populations (Wolfers et al. 2018; Elad et al. 2021). However, to our knowledge, no study has used the normative model to investigate the treatment effect in schizophrenia. Numerous studies have investigated the brain changes following both shortand long-term antipsychotic treatment, yet substantial heterogeneity is observed in the therapeutic effect (Zeng et al. 2021). A recently published metaanalysis examining 1280 individuals systematically synthesized and quantified the relationship between resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rs-fMRI) and antipsychotic response in schizophrenia. They found that based on pretreatment rsfMRI data, striatal and default mode network (DMN) functional segregation and integration metrics could predict treatment response with relatively high accuracy (Mehta et al. 2021). However, there was vast heterogeneity in the reviewed studies regarding patient characteristics. Recent studies also emphasize the importance of investigating the dynamic fluctuations of the brain networks. Including 140 drug-naïve schizophrenia patients and 119 HCs, a recent study found that patients exhibited impaired functional topology and increased temporal variability in dynamic FC in DMN, visual network (VN), and the somatomotor network (Tang et al. 2023). Another recent study also found altered static and dynamic connectivity properties after rTMS treatment, and the brain changes were associated with reduced severity of clinical symptoms (Xie et al. 2023). Traditionally, schizophrenia was characterized by positive symptoms and negative symptoms. To look at a broader spectrum of psychotic symptoms, and gain new insight into the predictive value of psychotic symptoms, a 5-factor consensus model of the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS) distinguishes between excited, disorganized, and depressive symptoms in addition to positive and negative symptoms were proposed (Wallwork et al. 2012). In the present study, to better depict the characteristics of different patients, we adopted this 5-factor model.
41:Previous studies have confirmed the utility of placing individuals into reference models. Motivated by the recent developments in normative models, this investigation aimed: (i) to use the Cambridge Centre for Ageing and Neuroscience (Cam-CAN) data set to build brain aging trajectories inferred from normative modelsoffunctional MRI data; (ii) to establish the brain-behaviors relationships, decompose the symptoms into 5 dimensions to test whether deviations from these models were related to psychiatric symptoms in patients with schizophrenia; (iii) to further investigated the pharmacological treatment effect on deviation distributions, and predict the deviation changes using baseline clinical variables.
49:## Demographic data of the patients with schizophrenia
51:The Ethics Committee of the Peking University Sixth Hospital approved the study. Forty healthy subjects and 42 patients with schizophrenia were enrolled and gave written informed consent according to the latest version of the Declaration of Helsinki. Five patients discontinued participation shortly before the first session. For the following data analysis, 37 subjects with a mean age of 28.41 yr (SD = 8.41) were included (for demographic characteristics at baseline, please see Table 1). The symptom severity of all schizophrenia patients was evaluated using the PANSS before and after the treatment by the same psychiatrist. All the patients received mono- or dual-therapy with antipsychotic medications for 6 wk. The antipsychotics were selected according to the individual patients' symptom characteristics. The antipsychotic medications are listed in the Supplementary Material. All patients with schizophrenia underwent 2 experimental sessions. The first session began before beginning antipsychotic treatment. The second session occurred on the last day of 6 wk of antipsychotic treatment. Each healthy volunteer underwent one experimental session.
55:The Cam-CAN data set provides detailed imaging data acquisition parameters, we also added this information to the Supplementary Materials. As for the data collected from patients with schizophrenia, the data acquisition parameters are presented below. Magnetic resonance imaging was performed using a Siemens Magnetom Trio 3.0 Tesla imaging system with a standard head coil at Peking University Third Hospital. The data collection details were as follows. For image registration, high-resolution structural T1 MRI data were acquired with the following acquisition parameters: 256 × 256 matrix size, 192 contiguous axial slices, 1 mm slice thickness, 1 × 1 × 1 mm 3 voxel resolution, 7 ◦ flip angle, 3.44 ms echo time, 2530 ms repetition time, and 1100 ms inversion time. Resting-state functional scans were obtained using a gradient-recalled echo-planar imaging sequence sensitive to blood oxygen-level-dependent contrast (2000 ms repetition time, 30 ms echo time, and 90 ◦ flip angle). The slice thickness was 4 mm (no gap) with a matrix size of 64 × 64 and a field of view of 220 × 220 mm 2 , resulting in a voxel size of 3.4 × 3.4 × 4.0 mm 3 . Each brain volume comprised 33 axial slices, and each functional run contained 240 image volumes.
57:Table 1. Demographic and clinical features of the participants in each group.
