95:The Cam-CAN provides us with the preprocessing neuroimaging data and the details are in the Supplementary Materials. For the imaging data of patients with schizophrenia and HCs, both structural and functional neuroimaging data were processed using spm toolbox in MATLAB (https://www.fil.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm/). The first 10 scans of the 240 dynamics were removed during the preprocessing. Spatial preprocessing involved reorienting to the imaginary line joining Anterior Commissure-Posterior Commissure, realignment, and slice-time correction. Brain tissue was segmented into gray matter/white matter/cerebrospinal fluid normalized to Montreal Neurological Institute space, and smoothed for spatial convolution with a 6-mm Gaussian kernel.
103:Based on the large public data set Cam-CAN, we estimated sexspecific normative age models for each dynamic FC feature. We used quantile linear regression to estimate the normative range (Allen et al. 2014; Jing et al. 2023; Lin et al. 2023). To quantify the normative model, we estimated the 50th (median) percentile regression curves with bootstrapping confidence intervals. To avoid bias in the model, we computed the normative range by estimating the 5th and 95th percentiles in regression models. The gap between the 5th and 95th percentiles for a given age was as a scaling coefficient to normalize the feature deviation (Jing et al. 2023; Lin et al. 2023). To avoid the potential sex effects on model construction, we separately estimated the normative model in female/male groups. Furthermore, to improve the generalizability of the model, we estimated the normative models of healthy individuals from Cam-CAN under 10-fold cross-validation. Specifically, the data were randomly divided into 10 folds. Nine-folds were utilized for building the model, whereas the remaining fold was employed to evaluate the general performance of the model. This process was repeated 10 times, with each fold being excluded once. Finally, 10 models were aggregated into normative models for dynamic FC features.
105:## Estimating individual features deviations in normative models for SCZ patients
111:dynamic (fFCS) FC, so 34 features represented each individual's dynamic FC pattern. Individualized deviation profiles of 68 features in SCZ patients (including before and after treatment, bSCZ:34, and aSCZ:34) were derived from sex-specific normative age models (separate models for female patients vs. male patients). For each patient, the features of the same age were positioned on the normative percentile curves from Cam-CAN to estimate individual deviation. We derived a Z -score with a scaling coefficient that quantified the deviation from the normative model for each feature as follows:
115:For each individual, index i represented individual's ID, and j represented one of 34 features. Yij was the observed value for the i th individual's j th feature. Y 50 ij , Y 95 ij , and Y 5 ij were the predictive features in 50th, 95th, and 5th percentiles for the same age, respectively. Thus, the z -score in formula presents the deviation of a certain individual's a certain feature and it is a statistical quantitative description of how much the patient alters from the healthy dynamic FC pattern. The average value of the 10 individual z -scores yielded from the aggregated normative models was the final features deviation for each patient.
117:## Characterizing bSCZ and aSCZ based on individual deviations and clinical response
119:General linear models and Pearson's correlations of the deviation profiles on bSCZ/aSCZ and clinical PANSS scores were conducted to explore the relationships between clinical symptom improvement and changes in dynamic FC patterns gaged with the deviation Z -scores. We used permutation tests ( n =5,000) to conduct the Pearson correlations to avoid false positive issues with sex, age, and head motion as covariates. The permutation correlation analysis is non-parametric, distribution-free, and particularly beneficial when dealing with a small sample size.

